>
	<header class="page-hero shell">
		<p class="eyebrow">Washington, DC · July 19, 2012</p>
		<h1>Three stolen bikes. One idea that traveled further.</h1>
		<p class="lede">In 2012, cyclist Alexander D. Mills wrote to the Metropolitan Police Department proposing a coordinated response to bicycle theft. The proposal began with his experience of three stolen bicycles and a simple question: could a connected, accountable response help riders recover their transportation?</p>
		<div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/city-program/">Explore the modern city framework</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/bait-bikes/bait-bikes.github.io/blob/main/docs/history/2012-dc-anti-bike-theft-proposal.md">Read the archived 2012 letter</a></div>
	</header>

	<section class="section light" id="original">
		<div class="shell">
			<div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">The original concept</p><h2>Local frustration became a proposed public partnership.</h2></div><p>The letter envisioned designated bicycles equipped with concealed GPS tracking, movement alerts, and timely coordination with MPD. It also proposed a supporting nonprofit to maintain bicycles and equipment, undertake prevention work, and coordinate with public institutions.</p></div>
			<div class="card-grid">
				<article class="card"><span class="number">THE PERSON</span><h3>Loss is more than property</h3><p>A bicycle can be a person's transport, exercise, independence, and investment; theft also takes a lock and time spent replacing both.</p></article>
				<article class="card"><span class="number">THE SYSTEM</span><h3>Response needs coordination</h3><p>The original design connected an asset record and tracker signal to an agency response, rather than treating a location dot as the entire solution.</p></article>
				<article class="card"><span class="number">THE INSTITUTIONS</span><h3>Prevention needs continuity</h3><p>City agencies, rider groups, bike shops, donors, and nonprofits can all have specific roles that outlast a single operation.</p></article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" id="historical-limits">
		<div class="shell split">
			<div class="prose">
				<p class="eyebrow">Read history as history</p>
				<h2>The 2012 numbers and assumptions are not contemporary evidence.</h2>
				<p>The original letter listed annual DC theft figures for 2007–2011 and estimated that most thefts went unreported. The sources were not supplied, the 2011 figure of five was questioned by the author, and the underreporting estimate was conjectural. We preserve them in the archive but do not present them as verified counts or current trends.</p>
				<p>The letter also speculated about existing agency funds, automatic search authority from GPS, fines covering operating costs, and 80–90% recovery or enforcement targets. None should be accepted without independent evidence, legal review, and a realistic budget. In particular, a tracker alert is an investigative lead—not a warrant, a finding of theft, or proof that the person with the bicycle is guilty.</p>
			</div>
			<aside class="aside-panel">
				<p class="eyebrow">Historical, not an endorsement</p>
				<h3>What the archive does—and does not—show</h3>
				<ul><li>Shows an authentic 2012 local proposal and its proposed model</li><li>Does not document MPD adoption, funding, or deployment</li><li>Does not verify theft totals or response rates</li><li>Does not replace modern legal, privacy, evidence, or community safeguards</li></ul>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section light" id="evolution">
		<div class="shell">
			<div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">The next iteration</p><h2>From a DC sting proposal to a reusable public-safety program.</h2></div><p>The most useful part of the original idea is the coordinated model: protect riders, match assets to lawful recovery workflows, and establish an organization that can sustain training, technology, measurement, and prevention.</p></div>
			<div class="steps">
				<article class="step"><div><h3>Start with locally measured harm</h3><p>Include reporting gaps, travel disruption, repair or replacement cost, community experience, and the limits of police incident data.</p></div></article>
				<article class="step"><div><h3>Choose the least intrusive effective intervention</h3><p>Secure racks, registration, owner support, shop coordination, and data-sharing improvements belong in the plan alongside any supervised bait asset.</p></div></article>
				<article class="step"><div><h3>Make alerts actionable, but never automatic authority</h3><p>Build incident verification, supervision, safe response, evidence handling, privacy boundaries, retention, and documented disengagement into operations.</p></div></article>
				<article class="step"><div><h3>Pay for service, not arrests</h3><p>Use transparent budgets, authorized contracts, grants, and donations with conflict-of-interest controls. Evaluate theft reduction, recoveries, safety, and trust—not fine revenue.</p></div></article>
			</div>
